       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written. Your confirmation doesn't change the risk. 9000 mg/day of acetaminophen is more than double the maximum daily dose, and a 3000 mg single dose is three times the usual maximum. That could cause serious liver injury or liver failure in your patient. I can't think of a standard indication that calls for it.\n\nHere are some options:\n- **Enter a standard dose now.** For example, I can enter acetaminophen 1000 mg PO three times daily (3000 mg/day), or 650 mg PO every 6 hours. Tell me which one and I'll enter it.\n- **Review the dose with your resident, attending, or pharmacist.** If a clinician has a specific reason for a higher dose, they should place or co-sign the order themselves. Pharmacy will likely flag it anyway.\n- **Check the source of the dose.** The number may have been mixed up with the total daily dose (3000 mg/day) or with the dose in a different formulation.\n\nWould you like me to enter one of the standard regimens?"
      }
     ],
